Remove the "Passwords and autofill" row from the profile (avatar) menu.

This is the fourth surface for the same feature and the last one still
reachable. aerium-remove-autofill-passwords-menu.patch took the entry out of
the chrome://settings left-hand navigation, and
aerium-remove-passwords-autofill-app-menu.patch took the three-dot app menu
submenu out; the bubble that opens from the avatar button kept its own row,
which called chrome::ShowSettingsSubPage(chrome::kAutofillSubPage) - straight
into the settings section that no longer exists. Clicking it landed the user
on a settings page with nothing on it.

Both click handlers go with the row. OnAutofillSettingsButtonClicked and
OnYourSavedInfoSettingsButtonClicked are the two arms of the same feature
switch inside BuildAutofillSettingsButton (autofill::features::
kYourSavedInfoSettingsPage picks between them); with the builder gone neither
is referenced any more, and both navigate to the same removed subpage, so
there is nothing to preserve. They are private members declared in the header,
so their declarations are removed there too - leaving a declaration without a
definition would be harmless but misleading, and leaving the definitions in
place would keep the two "Autofill.*SettingsPage.VisitReferrer" histograms
compiled in for events that can no longer happen.

The two components/autofill includes are dropped with them: after this patch
the file has no other autofill reference (autofill_settings_metrics.h was used
only by the two UmaHistogramEnumeration calls, autofill_features.h only by the
kYourSavedInfoSettingsPage check). base/metrics/histogram_functions.h stays -
ProfileChooser.HasProfilesShown still uses it.

ProfileMenuViewBase::ActionableItem::kAutofillSettingsButton is deliberately
left in place. It is an explicitly numbered histogram enum (= 22) tied to
tools/metrics/histograms/metadata/profile/enums.xml through a LINT.IfChange
block; logged enum values are never removed or renumbered, and removing it
would also break profile_menu_view_browsertest.cc, which references it in
roughly thirty expected-item lists. Those browsertests are not built in
release configurations, but they still have to keep compiling for anyone who
does build them - they will fail at runtime now that the row is gone, which is
the correct signal rather than something to paper over.

BuildAutofillSettingsButton is removed rather than emptied. It is a non-static
out-of-line member function, so clang would not warn about it being unused,
but an empty builder that BuildFeatureButtons no longer calls is dead weight;
the only reason to keep a stub would be an override or a caller in another
translation unit, and it is private with no such caller.

